---
// Parity: same inputs, two implementations, one tolerance.
---

<figure class="fig">
            <div style="border: 1px solid var(--rule); background: var(--card); padding: 20px; min-width: 0">
              <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: var(--f-dir); gap: 10px; align-items: stretch">
                <div style="flex: var(--f-item); border: 1px solid var(--rule); background: var(--paper); padding: 12px; display: grid; gap: 5px; align-content: start; min-width: 0; font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.4"><span style="font: 500 11.5px/1.4 var(--mono); color: var(--accent-ink)">1 · sample</span><strong style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem">About 200 items</strong><span style="color: var(--muted)">100 real and 100 fake, in the smoke namespace</span></div>
                <span aria-hidden="true" style="flex: none; align-self: center; font: 500 15px/1 var(--mono); color: var(--muted)"><i class="arr"></i></span>
                <div style="flex: var(--f-item); border: 1px solid var(--rule); background: var(--paper); padding: 12px; display: grid; gap: 5px; align-content: start; min-width: 0; font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.4"><span style="font: 500 11.5px/1.4 var(--mono); color: var(--accent-ink)">2 · parity.py export</span><strong style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem">The exact inputs</strong><span style="color: var(--muted)">Windows or crops from the shared loaders, and the adapter's scores</span></div>
                <span aria-hidden="true" style="flex: none; align-self: center; font: 500 15px/1 var(--mono); color: var(--muted)"><i class="arr"></i></span>
                <div style="flex: var(--f-item-w); display: grid; gap: 8px; min-width: 0">
                  <div style="border: 1px solid var(--ink); background: var(--paper); padding: 10px 12px; display: grid; gap: 3px; min-width: 0; font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.4"><span style="font: 500 11.5px/1.4 var(--mono); color: var(--muted)">ours</span><strong style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem">Our adapter</strong><span style="color: var(--muted)">Python 3.12, torch 2.8, the one shared environment</span></div>
                  <div style="border: 1px solid var(--ink); background: var(--paper); padding: 10px 12px; display: grid; gap: 3px; min-width: 0; font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.4"><span style="font: 500 11.5px/1.4 var(--mono); color: var(--muted)">upstream</span><strong style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem">The authors' code</strong><span style="color: var(--muted)">A reference script, in upstream's own environment where it differs (an old torch, its fairseq)</span></div>
                </div>
                <span aria-hidden="true" style="flex: none; align-self: center; font: 500 15px/1 var(--mono); color: var(--muted)"><i class="arr"></i></span>
                <div style="flex: var(--f-item); border: 1px solid var(--good); background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--good) 10%, var(--card)); padding: 12px; display: grid; gap: 5px; align-content: start; min-width: 0; font-size: 0.86rem; line-height: 1.4"><span style="font: 500 11.5px/1.4 var(--mono); color: var(--good)">3 · parity.py compare</span><strong style="font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.95rem">Every score within 1e-3</strong><span style="color: var(--ink-2)">A pass writes <code style="font: 400 0.92em var(--mono); overflow-wrap: anywhere">results/parity/&lt;id&gt;.json</code>, with no item ids</span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <figcaption><strong>Parity: same inputs, two implementations, one tolerance.</strong> A pass is what licenses every patch to the port. Score direction is a separate check, on a reproduction set.</figcaption>
          </figure>
